    "problem_statement": "# Connection.receive_data doesn't check for ERROR state\n\nWhen trying to receive data from a connection that is already in an ERROR state, the library doesn't properly prevent further operations.\n\n## Reproduction\n\nHere's a simple example that demonstrates the issue:\n\n```python\nfrom h11 import Connection, CLIENT, RemoteProtocolError\n\n# Create a connection\nconn = Connection(our_role=CLIENT)\n\n# Send invalid data to trigger an error\nconn.receive_data(b'gibberish\\r\\n\\r\\n')\n\n# This will raise a RemoteProtocolError and set their_state to ERROR\ntry:\n    conn.next_event()\nexcept RemoteProtocolError:\n    pass\n\n# This should fail but doesn't - the connection allows receiving more data\n# even though the peer is in ERROR state\nconn.receive_data(b'more data')\n```\n\nThe problem is that when a connection's peer is in the ERROR state, the library should prevent further receive operations, but it currently allows them. This can lead to unexpected behavior since processing should have stopped after the error was detected.\n\nThe connection should reject any attempts to receive more data once the peer state is ERROR, similar to how it rejects sending data when in an error state.\n\n<END WRITING>"
